    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/huns"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huns.jpg" alt="Huns" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hunnen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Peter Johann Nepomuk Geiger, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> were a nomadic people first reported east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga River</a>, in what was then Scythia; their origins are uncertain, probably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and classical sources say they appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> suddenly around 370. Since the 18th century historians have debated a link with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>, and there is no consensus. Roman writers equated them with earlier steppe peoples and caricatured them as monsters; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> records that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> held them to be the offspring of unclean spirits and witches.</p><p>Their history in the fourth century is unclear, and the Huns left no sources of their own. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> became aware of them when their invasion of the Pontic steppes forced thousands of Goths to seek refuge across the Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in 376. The Huns conquered the Alans, most of the Greuthungi or Eastern Goths, and then most of the Thervingi or Western Goths. In 395 they launched their first large-scale attack on the Eastern Roman Empire, striking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, overrunning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, pillaging Cappadocia and entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, while an invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> came close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> before a Persian counter-attack defeated it. Uldin, the first Hun named in contemporary sources, fought for the Romans against Radagaisus, beheaded the Goth Gainas around 400–401 and pillaged Thrace in 408, until the Romans bought off his subordinates and he vanished from history.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus.jpg" alt="Ammianus Marcellinus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ammianus_Marcellinus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Author of the original work of art is long dead, while the uploader of the picture portraying it released it under a CC BY 3.0 license., CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> practised pastoral nomadism within defined territories, moving between summer pastures and fixed winter quarters; Their herds held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, horses and goats, and sheep, whose bones fill Hun-period graves, must have been the greater part. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a> says most of their diet was meat; they drank mare's milk and probably made cheese and kumis. Ancient sources deny them any agriculture, but Hyun Jin Kim classes the Huns as agro-pastoralists. Elites practised polygamy, commoners probably monogamy; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscus">Priscus</a> saw women moving freely among men, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bleda">Bleda</a>'s widow commanded a village through which Roman envoys rode. Artificial cranial deformation marks 50 to 80 per cent of Hun-period burials in the Carpathian basin.</p><p>As contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> grew, the economy came to rest on tribute, plunder and trade. The Huns received great quantities of gold as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and as tribute, and Denis Sinor argued that under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> they depended almost entirely on plunder and Roman payments; Roman gold coins circulated throughout their empire, though the European Huns struck none of their own. Captives were ransomed or sold to Roman slave dealers, and slaves managed herds, served in households and, if educated, worked as administrators or architects. Trade with Rome was strictly regulated and, according to Priscus, held at a fair once a year, horses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and slaves going for weapons, linen, grain, wine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius.jpg" alt="Flavius Aetius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Flavius Aetius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diptych_Aetius.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tataryn, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>' main method of warfare was mounted archery. Ammianus describes them entering battle in wedge-shaped masses, fighting from a distance with arrows, then closing with swords and throwing plaited nooses to entangle their enemies. Their composite 'Hun-type' bow, 120 to 150 centimetres long, shot larger arrows with iron three-lobed heads; princes were buried with golden ceremonial bows from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. They used whips rather than spurs, and no stirrups have been found in their graves, so they preferred the bow to close combat. Armour was probably lamellar and rare, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreat</a> was a favourite stratagem. They <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fortified-camp">fortified camps</a> with wagons and could besiege walled cities, as in the campaign of 441.</p><p>Hunnic armies fought almost always alongside Germanic or Iranian subjects; at the Catalaunian Plains <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> placed his subject peoples on the wings and the Huns in the centre. They were not always effective in pitched battle, losing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> in 439, barely winning at the Utus in 447, probably losing or drawing at the Catalaunian Plains in 451 and losing at Nedao. War and its threat were tools to extort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> breached the Treaty of Margus in 440, Attila and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bleda">Bleda</a> razed Margus, Singidunum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viminacium">Viminacium</a>; in 443, after defeating the Romans at the Battle of Chersonesus, they forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> to the Peace of Anatolius. In 451 they besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flavius-aetius">Flavius Aetius</a> relieved it, and in 452 Attila sacked cities across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/attila"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/attila.jpg" alt="Attila" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Attila_Hunnorum_Rex_Flagellum_Dei_Aquilejae_Eversor_Utini_Instaurator.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Julio Strozza, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The structure of Hunnic rule is debated. Ammianus wrote that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> of his day had no kings but leading men for war, taking decisions in a general council on horseback. Peter Heather sees a loose confederation that developed a ranking hierarchy; E. A. Thompson argues that permanent kingship arose only with the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; Peter Golden holds that even under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> 'it can hardly be called a state, much less an empire'; Kim argues for a centralised order inherited from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>. The Huns usually had two rulers at once: Attila and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bleda">Bleda</a> ruled jointly from 434, and Attila appointed his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellac">Ellac</a> co-king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscus">Priscus</a> calls Attila king or emperor, but the native title is unknown.</p><p>Priscus also names five 'picked men', logades, in Attila's government, chosen by birth or merit, whom Thompson saw as the hinge of administration, each commanding detachments and collecting tribute from a part of the empire; Maenchen-Helfen thought them simply prominent individuals, and Kim suggests Roman defectors may have staffed a kind of bureaucracy. Subject peoples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a>, Sarmatians, Heruli, Alans, Rugii, Suevi and Sciri, kept their own kings, and those recognised as ethnic Huns had more rights. Of religion almost nothing is known: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> records the sword of Mars as Attila's title to rule the world, divination and probably shamans are attested, and Attila tolerated Nicene and Arian Christians among his subjects.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/goths"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/goths.jpg" alt="Goths" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ostgoten-fibel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> may have set the Great Migration in motion, a contributing factor in the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>. Their arrival on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> by 370 pushed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> and Alans westward and sent thousands of Goths over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in 376, and by 430 they had built a vast if short-lived empire on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s Danubian frontier under whose hegemony Goths, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a>, Sarmatians, Heruli, Suebi, Sciri and Rugii kept their own kingdoms. The eastern court paid them, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-margus">Treaty of Margus</a> in 435 to the 2,100 pounds of gold a year agreed in 449. Their rulers' prestige outlived them: post-Hunnic steppe peoples claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>, and Byzantine and western writers went on calling later steppe peoples 'Huns' for centuries.</p><p>Archaeologically the Huns left little that is certainly theirs: about 200 plausible burials as of 2005, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> cauldrons deposited near water, polychrome cloisonné jewellery and gold diadems. Their memory lived on in legend. Christian hagiography made them antagonists of saints: Attila turned back by Pope Leo I and the apostles, Saint Ursula and her virgins killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. Germanic heroic legend placed Attila at the centre of the Nibelung and Dietrich von Bern cycles. Medieval Hungarian chroniclers, first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-of-keza">Simon of Kéza</a> in 1282–1285, claimed the Hungarians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szekelys">Székelys</a> as the Huns' descendants, a fiction mainstream scholarship rejects but which still shapes Hungarian nationalism. Allied propaganda called Germans 'Huns' in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valentinian-iii.jpg" alt="Valentinian III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-iii">Valentinian III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Valentinian_III,_Louvre_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tony Querrec (photo), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>'s last years brought the empire to its height and its edge. He claimed Honoria, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valentinian-iii">Valentinian III</a>, as his bride with half the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> as dowry, and in 451 entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> fought him at the Catalaunian Plains. In 452 he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and razed cities in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> until the envoys Gennadius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avienus">Avienus</a>, Trigetius and Pope Leo I met him near Mantua and won his promise to withdraw. When the eastern emperor Marcian stopped the tribute, Attila planned to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, but in 453 he died of a haemorrhage on his wedding night. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a> describes the funeral: horsemen circling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> tent singing the strava, the coffin buried secretly and the slaves who dug the grave killed.</p><p>His death set the Germanic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> against the Hunnic ruling body. Ellak, his favoured son and ruler of the Akatziri, met the Gepid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardaric">Ardaric</a>'s coalition at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nedao">Battle of Nedao</a>, around 454, and the Amali <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> under Valamir revolted the same year; many Germanic vassals were lost and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> were driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>. New Oghur Turkic-speaking peoples arrived from the east, and in 463 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saragurs">Saragurs</a> defeated the Akatziri. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dengizich">Dengizich</a> attacked the Romans in 467 without his brother Ernak, was besieged, and was defeated by the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aspar">Aspar</a>. In 469 Dengizich was defeated and killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, and the Huns were absorbed by peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>; Kim argues they lived on under Ernak as the Kutrigur and Utigur Hunno-Bulgars.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, nomadic horse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> of uncertain origin, reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> by 370, drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and from the Hungarian Plain extorted gold from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, culminating under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> in the invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 451–452. His death in 453 loosed their Germanic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dengizich">Dengizich</a>'s death in 469 the Huns vanished into other peoples.</em></p>

